nning kill works well, cutting invented events from 150 to 117 and raising mean OVERALL F1 by +0.024 (gen_03/cand_0, 0.735 to 0.759).\n- Stacking action-pruning rules sequentially across generations (sets in gen 1 -> blocks/kills in gen 2 -> digs in gen 3) compounds gains cleanly, reducing invented events by more than half (from >230 to 117) and raising mean OVERALL F1 by +0.138 total (0.621 to 0.759).\n- Clarifying that deflected point-winning spikes are still rally-ending 'kills' and excluding soft free-ball passes from 'attacks' reached the highest single-run F1 of 0.777 (103 invented) and a 2-run mean of 0.766 (+0.007 over gen_03/cand_0), though it was rejected on confirmation (gen_04/cand_3).\n- Providing team jersey colors and valid roster numbers trades action/time precision for shirt number accuracy: in every generation it boosted number F1 (+0.014 in gen_02/cand_3, +0.043 in gen_03/cand_3, +0.019 in gen_04/cand_0) but degraded time/action F1 and increased invented events (e.g., 117 to 133 in gen_04/cand_0, dropping OVERALL F1 from 0.766 to 0.753).\n- Attempting to fix early serve timestamps via prompt instructions consistently fails to improve OVERALL F1 across all four generations, whether anchored to physical ball-hand contact (gen_01/cand_3: 0.653; gen_02/cand_2: 0.688), ~1s before receive (gen_03/cand_2: 0.736), or crossing the net (gen_04/cand_1: 0.752 vs 0.759 baseline).\n- Restricting 'attack' and 'kill' strictly to \"jumping overhead hits\" trades fewer invented events for worse recall, cutting inventions from 150 to 132 but increasing missed events from 39 to 46 and leaving OVERALL F1 flat at 0.735 (gen_03/cand_1).\n- Adding multi-contact sequence logic for digs (forbidding consecutive digs or digs by the attacking team) does not improve over simpler dig rules, slightly increasing invented events (117 to 121) and missed events (33 to 37) for an OVERALL F1 of 0.761 (gen_04/cand_2).\n- Ordering of rules matters for evaluation efficiency: applying dig